      "title": "Subagent granted the built-in Bash tool",
      "explanation": "This subagent's frontmatter grants the built-in `Bash` tool, letting it run arbitrary shell commands. A narrowly-scoped subagent (e.g. a search or read specialist) rarely needs shell access; granting it widens the blast radius if the subagent is compromised or misdirected.\n",
      "suggested_fix": "Remove `Bash` from the subagent's `tools:` list unless shell access is essential. Prefer specific tools (Read, Grep, Glob) for read-only roles.\n",
      "confidence": 0.9
    },
    {
      "rule_id": "CSDK-130",
      "category": "claude_sdk",
      "scope": "agent",
      "severity": "high",
      "tool_name": "AIClient.queryStream",
      "file_path": "ccsdk/ai-client.ts",
      "start_line": 86,
      "end_line": 89,
      "title": "TypeScript query() main agent is granted the Bash tool",
      "explanation": "This `query(...)` call configures the main agent thread with \"Bash\" in its `options.allowedTools`, granting the top-level agent arbitrary shell-command execution. The main thread acts directly on model output in the conversation loop, so a prompt-injected instruction reaches the OS shell with no subagent boundary in between. This is the broadest privilege a Claude TypeScript agent can hold, and unlike the AgentDefinition and `.claude/agents` subagent rules, nothing else inspects the `query()` main thread's tool grant.\n",
      "suggested_fix": "Remove \"Bash\" from `options.allowedTools` unless shell execution is essential to the agent's task. If it is, pair it with a PreToolUse hook that allowlists the exact commands permitted, and do not run the session under permissionMode: \"bypassPermissions\".\n",
      "confidence": 0.8
    },
    {
      "rule_id": "CSDK-131",
      "category": "claude_sdk",
      "scope": "agent",
      "severity": "high",
      "tool_name": "AIClient.queryStream",
      "file_path": "ccsdk/ai-client.ts",
      "start_line": 86,
      "end_line": 89,
      "title": "TypeScript query() main agent is granted filesystem-write or web-fetch built-ins",
      "explanation": "This `query(...)` main agent's `options.allowedTools` includes a filesystem-write built-in (Write/Edit/MultiEdit/NotebookEdit) or WebFetch. Write access lets a prompt-injected main agent rewrite source, configuration, or the `.claude/settings.json` that governs its own permissions; WebFetch pulls attacker-controllable URL content straight back into the loop, which is both a prompt-injection re-entry and a server-side request forgery surface. On the main thread these run with no subagent boundary and no SDK guardrail mechanism.\n",
